1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERA: So why isn't the sky violet?

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERB: There isn't one single reason. It takes a few things together.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERB: Part of it is that sunlight holds less violet than blue to begin with.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERA: And some of the violet is absorbed high in the atmosphere, before it reaches us.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERB: Still, a rainbow shows that plenty of violet gets through. So the rest of the answer is in our eyes.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERA: For one thing, our eyes are less sensitive to violet.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERB: And we see colour with three kinds of cone cells, which respond most strongly to red, green and blue light.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERA: Skylight stimulates the red and green cones about equally, and the blue cones more strongly.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERB: And our vision reads that combination as a pale blue.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERA: So the colour isn't only out there in the sky.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERB: No. The blue of the sky is partly physics, and partly the way we see.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERB: Here's another one: if air turns sunlight blue, why are clouds white?

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERA: Clouds are made of water droplets, typically a few micrometres, or thousandths of a millimetre, across.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERB: That still sounds tiny.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERA: It is, but it's thousands of times bigger than an air molecule, and bigger than a wave of light.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERB: And particles that big don't follow Rayleigh's rule. They scatter all colours about equally.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERA: That's called Mee scattering, after the German physicist Gustav Mee.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERB: And if you scatter every colour alike, white light stays white.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERA: A thick cloud reflects seventy to ninety-five percent of the light that falls on it, across all colours.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERB: Which is why the tops of big clouds look so bright.
